WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.650
I know the opportunities that came my way
as a, as a result of learning languages.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:10.320
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here today,
I want to talk about how language and

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.690
learning languages has changed my life.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:17.250
Um, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.870
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.250
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.230
You know, I was thinking about
this the other day, because here

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:31.110
I am, I'm 76 and, uh, when I was
17, I spoke English essentially.

00:00:32.129 --> 00:00:34.589
And, uh, then I got very
interested in French.

00:00:35.129 --> 00:00:36.450
And so how did that change my life?

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.980
It changed my life because I was
able to convert myself into a

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.480
fluent speaker of another language.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.170
I realized at the age of say
18 that that was possible.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.360
And then I ended up going to France.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.780
And so I experienced living in France
getting to know French culture.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.480
Uh, and sort of with that, I was
able to travel into Spain and get a

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.230
sense of Spanish, Spanish culture,
Spanish people, the country.

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:05.160
And then I went to China and
learned, or at least to Hong Kong,

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.429
and learned Mandarin Chinese,
and that's a whole other world.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.810
And then I lived in Japan and having
learned the Chinese characters I was

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.920
able to learn Japanese and lived there
for nine years and got into the culture,

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.330
particularly as it applies to wood
because I was in the wood business.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.040
And all of these things...

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.870
and then I could go on and on and on of
course, most recently, um, first of all,

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:31.980
learning Russian, uh, at the age of 60
and then moving from there into other

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:38.340
Slavic languages, discovering so much
about those cultures and those people.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.490
And more recently, my Persian and Arabic
again, which brings those people to life.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.550
And because I learn the language,
I get interested in the culture.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:51.180
I just finished reading a book
about an Iranian lady who won

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.940
the Nobel peace prize in 2003.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:58.875
I think her name is, uh, Edabi
and so, you know, how has

00:01:58.875 --> 00:02:00.945
language learning changed my life?

00:02:01.365 --> 00:02:03.915
So, you know, maybe I've
missed out on certain things.

00:02:03.915 --> 00:02:07.185
If I hadn't learned those languages,
maybe I would have learned something else

00:02:07.185 --> 00:02:09.405
that would have brought me great joy.

00:02:09.795 --> 00:02:14.295
So you never know about the opportunities
that you missed, but I know the

00:02:14.295 --> 00:02:18.105
opportunities that came my way as
a, as a result of learning language.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:19.109
Yeah.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:24.540
First of all, opportunities say
professionally, I was able to, I got

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.630
my job initially with the government
because I was fluent in French.

00:02:28.109 --> 00:02:35.700
Um, I was assigned to Hong Kong to
learn Mandarin Chinese by the, uh, trade

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:41.265
commissioner service and, um, so that
was connected with my, with my work.

00:02:41.265 --> 00:02:42.705
And then I, I worked in Japan.

00:02:42.705 --> 00:02:45.375
I got a job in Japan and I
built my business based on

00:02:45.375 --> 00:02:46.755
understanding the Japanese market.

00:02:47.055 --> 00:02:49.545
So there's all of that, but
it's much, much more than that.

00:02:49.875 --> 00:02:55.275
It's a perspective on different
cultures, as different as say, Korean,

00:02:55.305 --> 00:03:03.465
Chinese, Russian uh, Brazilian, uh, you
know, Greek, Romanian and, and having

00:03:03.494 --> 00:03:09.135
touched, you know, in, you know, very
superficially or in greater depth, each

00:03:09.165 --> 00:03:14.685
of these cultures that make up the 20
languages that I have either learned or

00:03:14.685 --> 00:03:25.785
dabbled in, it brings me so much, uh,
perspective, um, you know, uh, a sense

00:03:25.785 --> 00:03:30.960
of participating in so many different
ways of, of looking at our world.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:38.010
So learning languages and one leads
to another has fundamentally changed

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:45.420
my life, whether that was the best
possible course that I could have

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:47.640
followed in my life who knows.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.290
You lead the life that you lead.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:56.790
But, uh, certainly for me, languages has
been an enormous part of my life and the

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:03.990
opportunity to, uh, you know, start LingQ,
to work on LingQ with my son and develop

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:06.510
a platform where people learn languages.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:12.330
We have 42 languages there now for people
to learn and as a platform, which I use to

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.430
learn and to continue learning languages.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:20.520
And you know, here I am, it's my third
career at age 76, I can't wait every

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:27.465
day to be able to get, you know, my
Arabic or my, uh, Persian and, uh, even

00:04:27.465 --> 00:04:31.825
to get to know a little bit about, you
know, Egyptian Arabic versus Levantine

00:04:31.845 --> 00:04:37.335
Arabic versus Modern Standard Arabic,
which were just terms for me before.

00:04:37.335 --> 00:04:40.815
But now that I get into those
languages, I see what's, what's behind

00:04:40.815 --> 00:04:44.655
those terms and the cultures that
are behind those terms and so forth.

00:04:44.655 --> 00:04:49.755
So I just thought I'd go through that
because you know, a lot of people learn

00:04:49.755 --> 00:04:55.965
languages for very practical reasons um,
English is the most popular language.

00:04:55.965 --> 00:04:59.145
At least the one that has learned by or
studied by the greatest number of people,

00:04:59.145 --> 00:05:04.455
because it's the most important sort of
international language of communication.

00:05:05.175 --> 00:05:09.345
But, uh, if you follow my channel, you
know that I always encourage people

00:05:09.345 --> 00:05:10.665
to learn more than one language.

00:05:11.145 --> 00:05:16.005
And the benefit is not only the
enjoyment of, of the learning process

00:05:16.005 --> 00:05:18.315
itself, but also what you come up with.

00:05:19.095 --> 00:05:22.485
Even if you end up forgetting some of
the languages that you have learned,

00:05:22.845 --> 00:05:26.595
but at least for the time that you were
learning those languages and discovering

00:05:26.595 --> 00:05:31.385
those languages, you were entering,
entering into a, another world, uh, a

00:05:31.395 --> 00:05:35.865
rich world that up to that point was
relatively unknown, at least to me.

00:05:36.555 --> 00:05:38.655
So just a brief ramble.

00:05:38.655 --> 00:05:44.725
I was just thinking about how lucky I
was that back some, you know, 50...60,

00:05:44.815 --> 00:05:51.075
60 years ago, I got totally wrapped up
in learning French, which then led to

00:05:51.075 --> 00:05:55.275
another and yet another and yet another,
and I'm very happy that I did that.

00:05:55.425 --> 00:05:58.755
So just thought I would share
those thoughts with you.

00:05:59.175 --> 00:06:00.115
Thank you for listening.

